  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cluster of thunderstorms will continue to sweep across \nnorth/central Lower Michigan along the lead edge of an instability \ngradient and warm front early this morning. There is a respectable \nwarm layer aloft over southeast Michigan per 00Z DTX RAOB last \nevening with mesoanalysis showing very limited surface instability. \nThis should help limit overall convective potential as the cluster of\nthunderstorms move through. Mid-level lapse rates with 20-30 knots \nof effective bulk shear will still allow for any stronger updrafts to\npose an isolated damaging wind threat as this lead wave works \nthrough the Saginaw Valley and Thumb. The arrival of deeper moisture \nwill also support brief heavy downpours.\n\nEvening suite of hi-res models are struggling to capture the lack of \nconvection with southern extent as several have much higher coverage \nthat reaches southern areas of southeast Michigan. Thus, the \nisolated severe threat looks confined along and north of I-69 \nthrough the remainder of the early morning hours. There is a \nsecondary weak wave that may move out of northern IL that may lead to\nan expansion of showers across southern portions of the CWA towards \n12Z, but confidence is low with how this plays out. Focus then turns\nto the trailing shortwave moving out of Minnesota and through \nWisconsin throughout the morning. This wave and associated cold front\nshould lead to better erosion of this mid level warm layer and \nprovide an opportunity for an uptick in convection behind morning \nconvection as it arrives towards the early afternoon. Increasing \ninstability profiles with more favorable diurnal timing and better \nCAPE depth with bulk shear of 20+ knots allows for a chance at \nisolated strong to possibly severe thunderstorm development. Activity\nwill pose mainly a damaging wind threat with a chance for small hail\nas well. Favored areas will include areas east of the I-75 corridor,\nmainly during the early to mid afternoon period. Aside from \nthunderstorm potential, expect westerly winds to increase throughout \nthe morning with breezy northwest winds behind the front later today \nas cold and dry air advection commences.\n\nTemperatures fall to well below average for July standards as 850 mb \ntemperatures dip into the single digits C bringing daytime highs for \nWednesday in the low to mid 70s. Comfortable humidity also expected \nwith dewpoints to to around 50 degrees. These dry and cool \nconditions will carry over into Thursday. High pressure continues to \nlimit convective potential into Friday, but southerly flow will \nbegin to kick in to close out the work week. This will a slight \nmoderation in the cooler airmass and help lift daytime highs up to \naround 80 degrees. Next prospects for precipitation look to be \nduring the later half of the weekend when a wave moves out of the \nnorthern plains along the northern periphery of the SW CONUS ridge.",
